    <h1 class="page-title">Creating a Jenkins Job & Git Integration</h1>

    <div class="note-card">
      
        <h2>What is a Job?</h2>
        <p>A <strong>Job</strong> in Jenkins is used to perform a set of tasks. Jenkins jobs are of two types:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Freestyle</strong> &mdash; the older, GUI-driven style.</li>
            <li><strong>Pipeline</strong> &mdash; the newer, script-based style.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Basic Steps to Create a Job</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Click on <strong>"New Item"</strong> / Create a job.</li>
            <li>Enter the task name.</li>
            <li>Choose <strong>Freestyle project</strong> or <strong>Pipeline</strong> depending on your requirement.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Connecting a Git Repository</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Copy the GitHub repo URL and paste it under <strong>Source Code Management &rarr; Git</strong>.</li>
            <li>If Git isn't installed on the server yet, install it on the AWS terminal:
                <pre><code>yum install git -y</code></pre>
            </li>
            <li>For a <strong>public</strong> repo, no credentials are needed. For a <strong>private</strong> repo, credentials must be created (covered in the parameters/credentials topic).</li>
            <li>To build from a specific branch, mention the branch name under <strong>Branches to build</strong>. By default Jenkins uses <code>*/master</code>.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Running the First Build</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Click <strong>Save</strong> and then <strong>Build Now</strong>.</li>
            <li>Check the output via <strong>Console Output</strong> (click the green tick mark in Build History).</li>
            <li>To see the repo files on the Linux server:
                <pre><code>cd /var/lib/jenkins/workspace/task_name</code></pre>
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Important:</strong> If you edit code on GitHub, the change will <em>not</em> reflect on the Linux server until you run the build again &mdash; this is a fully manual step unless we set up automatic triggers.</p>
        
    </div>

